<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gabon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gabonese Republic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gabon is a country on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a>, lying on the equator and bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equatorial-guinea">Equatorial Guinea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>. Its original inhabitants were the Bambenga, and Bantu migrants began settling the area in the 13th century BCE. The Kingdom of Orungu was established around 1700, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> colonised the region in the late 19th century. Gabon became independent from France in 1960. Omar Bongo came to office in 1967 and created a dynasty that held power through a client network, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francafrique">Françafrique</a>. A multi-party system was introduced in the 1990s, and the Gabonese Democratic Party stayed dominant until the 2023 coup.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Loango was created by Kongo people on the Atlantic coast north of the more powerful Kingdom of Kongo, in what is now the western Republic of the Congo, southern <b>Gabon</b> and Cabinda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Africa saw a new wave, with coups in Sudan in 2019 and 2021, Mali in 2020 and 2021, Guinea in 2021, Burkina Faso in 2022, and Niger and <b>Gabon</b> in 2023.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
